# The Event Sourcing Paradigm

At this point we have a barebones Phoenix project, with a rooms table.  Now what?

Let's take a step back and examine the hard problem we're trying solve.  Each client/player (I will use client and player interchangeably) will visit the "show" path for the room `/rooms/:id` and expect to "see" something: A state of the world.  They will also expect to "see" each other.  And when players manipulate the environment (pick up and throw an object, kill a monster, open a door, pickup a key), we expect other players to see those changes in their own respective browsers.  So the big problem definition we have is a syncronization problem between the clients.  How do we provide all clients with the current state of the world, take input changes from all clients, and syncronize those changes back out to each client?

This sounds like a job for event sourcing.

Event sourcing is simply recording all events as facts in a cronological ledger.  This historical record of all the facts is a "stream" of data called the event stream.  All clients are constantly producing events, just by entering the room and moving around. And all clients are also receiving the collective stream of room events so that they can update their local simulation of the world.  


## Defining events schema

Our events will have an event name and a payload for the event.  

Here are some examples of events that look like they could be published by a client:

```javascript
"item_picked_up", { "user" => "bob", "item" => "cup5" }
"user_pressed_switch", { "user" => "tom", "switch" => "door_trigger" }
"user_moved", { "user" => "bob", "position" => [1,2,3] }
```

These events consist of an event_name and some kind of payload.  This seems pretty flexible and straight forward.  It even tells a story and if we read from beginning to end we can replay an entire scene.

It will also be useful to be able to order events (we'll add timestamps) and also a sequence number in case two events arrive at the same time.  Finally all events belong to to a room so we'll have a room_id.  But all of these events can be added on the server side.

## Send events somewhere to be saved

To publish events within the server side, we can just use Phoenix PubSub and send a tuple {event_name, payload}.  

```elixir
PubSub.broadcast(Xr.PubSub, "room_stream:123", {"user_moved", %{"user_id" => "tom", "pose" => ...}})
```
In this example "room_stream:123" is the name of a topic.  And to subscribe and receive events from a particular topic we can just do this in a process:

```elixir
PubSub.subscribe(Xr.PubSub, "room_stream:123")
```

We're going to create a genserver at `lib/xr/servers/event_dispatcher.ex` to subscribe to the PubSub topic for a room.  Then batch the events every 100ms to a database table of events.  The GenServer will use a local registry so that the GenServer cannot be started twice.  We'll also create a supervisor to supervise the genserver.  Here is the finished code snippets.


## Add Local Registry for Unique GenServer Names

This GenServer is un-named right now, so we can't talk to it unless we have its pid.  But let's say we don't know its pid and we'd like to lookup this particular GenServer by a name.  A registry let's us do that.  The registry inserts a mapping between a name and a pid and also ensures that we won't accidentally start two of the same servers for the same room.  

Create a Registry by adding this line in the `applications.ex` children's list after the `Endpoint`:

```elixir
 {Registry, keys: :unique, name: Xr.RoomsRegistry},
```

Now we can name each process that is created from any GenServer we create using the `via_tuple` API.  Add this via_tuple function to our GenServer

## Add Event Dispatcher GenServer

Create a new folder at `lib/xr/servers` where we'll put our genserver code.

At a new file there called `event_dispatcher.ex` and paste the following:

```elixir
defmodule Xr.Servers.EventDispatcher do
  use GenServer
  alias Phoenix.PubSub

  # 100ms or 10 times per second
  @flush_interval 100

  def via_tuple(room_id) do
    {:via, Registry, {Xr.RoomsRegistry, "event_dispatcher:#{room_id}"}}
  end

  def start_link(room_id) do
    GenServer.start_link(__MODULE__, {:ok, room_id}, name: via_tuple(room_id))
  end

  def init({:ok, room_id}) do
    # subscribe to the room stream
    PubSub.subscribe(Xr.PubSub, "room_stream:#{room_id}")

    # create interval timer

    :timer.send_interval(@flush_interval, self(), :flush)

    # get max sequence from events table
    last_sequence = Xr.Rooms.max_sequence(room_id) || 0

    {:ok, %{room_id: room_id, events: [], next_sequence: last_sequence + 1}}
  end

  # responds to incoming message from the room stream
  def handle_info({event_name, payload}, state) do
    state = %{
      state
      | next_sequence: state.next_sequence + 1,
        events: [
          %{
            sequence: state.next_sequence,
            event_name: event_name,
            payload: payload,
            inserted_at: DateTime.utc_now(),
            updated_at: DateTime.utc_now()
          }
          | state.events
        ]
    }

    {:noreply, state}
  end

  def handle_info(:flush, state) do
    Xr.Rooms.insert_events(state.room_id, state.events)

    {:noreply, %{state | events: []}}
  end
end

```

## Add Supervisor

Here is the supervisor at `lib/xr/servers/rooms_supervisor.ex`:

```elixir
defmodule Xr.Servers.RoomsSupervisor do
  use DynamicSupervisor

  def start_link(_arg) do
    DynamicSupervisor.start_link(__MODULE__, :ok, name: __MODULE__)
  end

  def init(:ok) do
    DynamicSupervisor.init(strategy: :one_for_one)
  end

  def start_room(room_id) do
    DynamicSupervisor.start_child(__MODULE__, {Xr.Servers.EventDispatcher, room_id})
  end

  def stop_room(room_id) do
    DynamicSupervisor.terminate_child(
      __MODULE__,
      Xr.Servers.EntitiesDiff.via_tuple(room_id) |> GenServer.whereis()
    )

    DynamicSupervisor.terminate_child(
      __MODULE__,
      Xr.Servers.EntitiesState.via_tuple(room_id) |> GenServer.whereis()
    )
  end
end


```


Here is a corresponding test that illustrates that this GenServer stashes events into its state right now:

```elixir
defmodule Xr.Servers.EventDispatcherTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: true

  alias Xr.Servers.EventDispatcher
  alias Phoenix.PubSub

  test "receives and stores events in its state" do
    room_id = "abc"

    # Start the EventDispatcher GenServer
    {:ok, pid} = EventDispatcher.start_link(room_id)

    # Broadcast an event
    Phoenix.PubSub.broadcast(
      Xr.PubSub,
      "room_stream:#{room_id}",
      {"user_moved", %{"user_id" => "tom2", "position" => [1, 2, 3]}}
    )

    # Broadcast another event
    Phoenix.PubSub.broadcast(
      Xr.PubSub,
      "room_stream:#{room_id}",
      {"user_moved", %{"user_id" => "bob", "position" => [11, 12, 13]}}
    )

    # Allow some time for the message to be processed
    :timer.sleep(100)

    # Fetch the state of the GenServer
    state = :sys.get_state(pid)

    assert state.room_id == room_id
    assert state.next_sequence == 2
    assert length(state.events) == 2

    event = hd(state.events)
    assert event.sequence == 1
    assert event.event_name == "user_moved"
    assert event.payload == %{"user_id" => "bob", "position" => [11, 12, 13]}
  end
end
```


## Create the Events table

This generator will create an events table and add it under the rooms context.  It will also create some basic HTML CRUD pages, but we won't end up using most of them.

```bash
mix phx.gen.html Rooms Event events room_id:references:rooms sequence:integer event_name:string payload:map --binary-id
```

Make the following changes to the migration file:

```elixir
defmodule Xr.Repo.Migrations.CreateEvents do
  use Ecto.Migration

  def change do
    create table(:events, primary_key: false) do
      add :id, :binary_id, primary_key: true
      add :sequence, :integer, null: false, default: 0
      add :event_name, :string, null: false
      add :payload, :map, default: %{}
      add :room_id, references(:rooms, on_delete: :delete_all, type: :string)

      timestamps(type: :utc_datetime_usec)
    end

    create index(:events, [:room_id, :sequence], unique: true)
  end
end


```

Make the following changes to the schema file:

```elixir
defmodule Xr.Rooms.Event do
  use Ecto.Schema
  import Ecto.Changeset

  @primary_key {:id, :binary_id, autogenerate: true}
  @foreign_key_type :binary_id
  schema "events" do
    field :payload, :map
    field :sequence, :integer
    field :event_name, :string
    field :room_id, :string

    timestamps(type: :utc_datetime_usec)
  end

  @doc false
  def changeset(event, attrs) do
    event
    |> cast(attrs, [:sequence, :event_name, :payload])
    |> validate_required([:sequence, :event_name])
  end
end


```

Run the migration:

```bash
mix ecto.migrate
```

## Modify GenServer to batch save 

Now we have a table.  Let's modify the GenServer to periodically stash the events into the events table.  The generator that we ran earlier created some functions in the rooms context.  Most of them we do not need. 

Edit the rooms.ex context file and remove the update and delete event functions so that you're left with this:

```elixir

  alias Xr.Rooms.Event

  @doc """
  Returns the list of events.

  ## Examples

      iex> list_events()
      [%Event{}, ...]

  """
  def list_room_events(room_id) do
    Repo.all(from e in Event, where: e.room_id == ^room_id, order_by: e.sequence)
  end

  @doc """
  Batch insert a list of events
  """

  def insert_events(_room_id, []) do
    :noop
  end

  def insert_events(room_id, attributes_for_events) do
    # add a room_id for all events
    events = Enum.map(attributes_for_events, fn e -> Map.put(e, :room_id, room_id) end)
    Repo.insert_all(Event, events)
  end

  def max_sequence(room_id) do
    Repo.one(from e in Event, where: e.room_id == ^room_id, select: max(e.sequence))
  end
```

When we list events it only makes sense to do so in the context of a room, so we replaced the list_events function with a list_room_events function that takes a room_id.  
Be sure to also remove all the event tests from the rooms test file and fix the function name in the EventController.

```elixir
defmodule XrWeb.EventController do
  use XrWeb, :controller

  alias Xr.Rooms

  def index(conn, %{"room_id" => room_id}) do
    events = Rooms.list_room_events(room_id)
    render(conn, :index, events: events)
  end
end

```

